      "name": "What is the main difference between solvent-based and aqueous degreasers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Solvent-based degreasers use organic solvents — hydrocarbons, chlorinated solvents, oxygenated solvents, or blends — to dissolve oils and greases through direct chemical solubility. The soil literally dissolves into the solvent phase. Aqueous degreasers use water as the carrier and rely on alkaline chemistry, surfactant emulsification, saponification of fatty oils, and chelation of inorganic deposits to remove soils without dissolving them. Solvent degreasers excel at removing heavy mineral oils and difficult compounded soils quickly, with minimal substrate sensitivity, but carry VOC, flammability, and disposal penalties. Aqueous degreasers offer lower environmental impact, better regulatory acceptance, and lower operating cost, but require correct pH selection, rinsing, and drying steps to avoid substrate damage and water staining. The correct choice depends on the soil type, substrate material, cleanliness standard required, and the regulatory and operational constraints of the facility." }
