      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is saponification and how does it relate to degreasing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Saponification is the alkaline hydrolysis of fatty acid esters — including animal and vegetable oils, grease-based lubricants, and some synthetic esters — into water-soluble soaps and glycerol. In alkaline degreasers at elevated temperatures, the hydroxide ions react with the ester linkages in fatty-based soils to break them apart, producing surfactant-like fatty acid salts (soaps) that are themselves water-dispersible. Saponification is a chemical removal mechanism as distinct from physical emulsification: the soil is chemically transformed rather than physically dispersed. This makes alkaline aqueous degreasers particularly effective on vegetable oil-based machining aids, fatty acid drawing compounds, and animal-fat-contaminated surfaces. Mineral oils, synthetic esters with no saponifiable ester bonds, and wax-based soils do not saponify and must be removed by the emulsification mechanism instead." }
